A||N||10||Homicidal||HELP!  TERRIBLE Fiero problem!  Lost ALL acceleration!?!?!HELP!||||9||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-02-2000||04:51 AM||||To start off, I have an 84' Pontiac Fiero.  Runs great (or did), no previous problems.  <P>Okay, I was on the freeway, in 4th gear(stick-shift) going about 70, when all of a sudden all my acceleration left.  Gas pedal did nothing.  <P>Now everything happened quickly, but from what I remember, I tried downshifting into 3rd (going 70) and I remember getting life into the car, but when I dropped back into 4th (had to, or else would have blown the engine), the car had no acceleration again!  I pulled off the road, and when I got to the side, the car was dead.  <P>Now here I am:  I try and turn the car over, it turns over and gets the to point where it JUST is about to catch and then doesn't.  It just keeps chugging away.  Like if you had a slightly dead battery.<P>I have tried jumping it, nothing.  I've cleaned the carberator(sp?), nothing.  Checked spark plugs, etc..<P>What is wrong with my car?  Why will it not catch?  Normally I would say starter, but if it was the starter, it wouldn't even turn over.<P>Then I say fuel system.  But I'm getting the fuel, I checked it.  The little gas thingy IS shooting the gas into the engine. <P>So the only thing I can think of is the alternator.  But would that make me me lose all acceleration and prevent the car from starting?  I dunno, I'm lost.<P>Actually, it COULD be a transmission problem, right?  I HOPE NOT!<P>I also heard (just recently) it could be an electronic pick-up in the distributor.  I am so lost!||||reg||9
Z||000001||Uaana||04-02-2000||05:14 AM||||I know you said you checked your plugs, how?<BR>are you getting juice from the coil?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||04-02-2000||05:17 AM||||Just checked to see if they were cracked or something.  How would I go about testing them more in depth?  I'm not a big car buff, but if it can save me $$$, I'll become one QUICK!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Just for the info, I haven't had ANY symptoms of anything. I've had PERFECT revving, the car NEVER hesitates on startup before this, it's had GREAT pickup, etc.. So this can't be TOO major!! <P>It's just so frustrating, it was running PERFECT! Now it won't even start! SOMETHING had to have come loose or something.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Haze_Performance||04-02-2000||05:28 AM||||Last year I was at the (street) races and a 87 Fiero (bone stock) had the same thing happen to it.. His was a bad starter. I dont know if thats whats wrong with yours.. good luck..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||04-02-2000||05:35 AM||||But if it was a bad starter, would it turn over and crank like it does?  I heard a bad starter would just "click" everytime you turn the key.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Luperman||04-02-2000||09:45 AM||||you need to see if your getting spark out of the coil by pulling the center wire off your distributor cap and hold it about a 1/4 inch away from the block and look for blue spark, but I read earlier you checked for fuel flow  so be careful you don't want to start a fire that would be like icing on the cake .lol, If you have no spark it is very possible your ignition module and or pick-up coil is bad they are located in the distributor. hope this helps good luck||||reg||3
Z||000006||Old Lar||04-02-2000||09:58 AM||||You can drive without an alternator just on the battery for a while, so I'd rule out that. I had a fuel pump fuse blow which acted just like you described, but if you are getting fuel, rule out that.  I'd still check fuses first.  I've had bad wires, which cause a missing engine.  I've had a corroded coil wire, which caused a missing engine. <P>  Going dead on the road like that, timing belt(?) had that happen on two non Fiero cars, short in the distributor, my 84 Escort was famous for that.<BR> <BR>Sounds electrical.  Good Luck.<P>||||reg||3
Z||000007||RFMike||04-02-2000||10:11 AM||||I'm with Luperman.  Check for spark as described.  It is not the starter or transmission.<BR>There are several possibilities, but the pickup coil would be my first guess.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Homicidal||04-02-2000||01:33 PM||||UPDATE:<P>Okay, I checked my distributor cap.  There were 5 plugs, 4 around and one center.  I pulled off the center one to see it was severly corroded.  I checked the other and the top left one was also corroded.  I checked for spark and got nothing.  We even held a screwdriver up to it, nothing.<P>So I am figuring a bad distributor cap or just one that has been so corroded it won't start. <P>Any other possiblities?||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||04-02-2000||05:05 PM||||in your case,<P>replace the cap, rotor and wires.<P>also replace the control module on the distributor and the pickup coil in the distributor.<P>Use Borg Warner or ACDelco parts for everything. (There are good brands of aftermarket wires. Accell, MSD and others.)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ed||04-02-2000||07:55 PM||||I agree with Ogre.  And I would add one thing.<P>My (former) '84 died dead at an intersection once. All it needed was a tune-up. Funny how these computer controlled cars run fine until they don't.  Also had a sudden death in my V-6 due to failed ignition module.  <P>If it has been warning you a little before now, I'd also check the "cat".  ||||reg||1
